---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Answer from '../components/Answer.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is 1/4″ QC the same as 1/4″ NPT?', a: 'No. 1/4″ NPT is a tapered thread used on some gun bodies and adapters. 1/4″ QC is a plug/socket pair. You can buy NPT↔QC adapters.' },
  { q: 'Why did my plastic socket split?', a: 'Consumer kits use plastic collars on 1/4″ sockets. Brass or stainless lasts. Replace the socket before you replace the wand.' },
];
---
<Base title="1/4 inch quick-connect" description="1/4 inch quick-connect is the consumer standard for pressure-washer nozzles, foam cannons, and many surface cleaners." path="/qc-14/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/quick-connect/">Quick-connect</a> / 1/4″</p>
    <h1>1/4″ quick-connect</h1>
    <Answer>
      This is the plug on colored nozzle tips. If a foam cannon or surface cleaner “doesn’t fit the gun,” it usually wants this plug and you have an M22 wand.
    </Answer>
    <h2>Where it belongs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Snap-in tips (0°, 15°, 25°, 40°, soap)</li>
      <li>Most consumer foam cannons</li>
      <li>Many 12–15 in surface-cleaner inlets</li>
      <li>Short extension wands sold as “quick-connect”</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Where it does not belong</h2>
    <p>Do not use a 1/4″ QC as the only joint between a 3/8″ ID hose and a 4000 PSI gas pump if the coupler is a $6 plastic socket. Put 3/8″ QC (or M22) at the hose, and keep 1/4″ at the business end.</p>
    <p>See also <a href="/nozzles/">nozzle colors and orifice</a> and <a href="/foam-cannon/">foam-cannon inlets</a>.</p>
    <Faq items={faqs} />
  </div>
</Base>
